{"parentUuid":"80bddba7-2fa9-495e-8fdd-b70dfcd67839","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"3823edb3-8c6b-4f24-a573-c087008d6410","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"virtual-rolling-wilkes","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01H2kHEBhdikb7vxFyrPfTMw","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\n     2→import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Powerset\n     3→import Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\n     4→import Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n     5→\n     6→open BigOperators Finset\n     7→\n     8→/--\n     9→A finite set A of natural numbers has **distinct subset sums** if for any two\n    10→subsets S₁, S₂ ⊆ A, equality of their sums implies S₁ = S₂.\n    11→-/\n    12→def DistinctSubsetSums (A : Finset ℕ) : Prop :=\n    13→  ∀ S₁ ∈ A.powerset, ∀ S₂ ∈ A.powerset,\n    14→    S₁.sum id = S₂.sum id → S₁ = S₂\n    15→\n    16→/--\n    17→Erdős Problem #1 [Er56, Er57, Er61, Er65b, Er73, ErGr80, Er98]:\n    18→\n    19→If A ⊆ {1,…,N} with |A| = n is such that the subset sums ∑_{a ∈ S} a are\n    20→distinct for all S ⊆ A, then N ≫ 2ⁿ, i.e., there exists an absolute constant\n    21→C > 0 such that N ≥ C · 2ⁿ.\n    22→\n    23→The powers of 2 show that 2ⁿ would be best possible. Erdős called this\n    24→\"perhaps my first serious problem\" (dating it to 1931). The best known lower\n    25→bound is N ≥ C(n, ⌊n/2⌋), proved by Dubroff, Fox, and Xu (2021).\n    26→-/\n    27→theorem erdos_problem_1 :\n    28→    ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧\n    29→      ∀ (N n : ℕ) (A : Finset ℕ),\n    30→        (∀ a ∈ A, 1 ≤ a ∧ a ≤ N) →\n    31→        A.card = n →\n    32→        DistinctSubsetSums A →\n    33→        (N : ℝ) ≥ C * 2 ^ n :=\n    34→  sorry\n    35→"}]},"uuid":"f55a8541-315c-4e79-8947-3f2e62224e3b","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:17:51.407Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/1.lean","content":"import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Data.Finset.Powerset\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n\nopen BigOperators Finset\n\n/--\nA finite set A of natural numbers has **distinct subset sums** if for any two\nsubsets S₁, S₂ ⊆ A, equality of their sums implies S₁ = S₂.\n-/\ndef DistinctSubsetSums (A : Finset ℕ) : Prop :=\n  ∀ S₁ ∈ A.powerset, ∀ S₂ ∈ A.powerset,\n    S₁.sum id = S₂.sum id → S₁ = S₂\n\n/--\nErdős Problem #1 [Er56, Er57, Er61, Er65b, Er73, ErGr80, Er98]:\n\nIf A ⊆ {1,…,N} with |A| = n is such that the subset sums ∑_{a ∈ S} a are\ndistinct for all S ⊆ A, then N ≫ 2ⁿ, i.e., there exists an absolute constant\nC > 0 such that N ≥ C · 2ⁿ.\n\nThe powers of 2 show that 2ⁿ would be best possible. Erdős called this\n\"perhaps my first serious problem\" (dating it to 1931). The best known lower\nbound is N ≥ C(n, ⌊n/2⌋), proved by Dubroff, Fox, and Xu (2021).\n-/\ntheorem erdos_problem_1 :\n    ∃ C : ℝ, 0 < C ∧\n      ∀ (N n : ℕ) (A : Finset ℕ),\n        (∀ a ∈ A, 1 ≤ a ∧ a ≤ N) →\n        A.card = n →\n        DistinctSubsetSums A →\n        (N : ℝ) ≥ C * 2 ^ n :=\n  sorry\n","numLines":35,"startLine":1,"totalLines":35}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"80bddba7-2fa9-495e-8fdd-b70dfcd67839"}
