{"parentUuid":"0b3933bf-9424-4362-a88b-a57ba912d4d7","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"3823edb3-8c6b-4f24-a573-c087008d6410","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"virtual-rolling-wilkes","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014oPqWutpefruCWQNgMaNhg","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\n     2→import Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\n     3→import Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n     4→\n     5→open BigOperators Finset\n     6→\n     7→/--\n     8→The set A ⊆ ℕ has divergent reciprocal sum, i.e., ∑_{n ∈ A} 1/n = ∞.\n     9→Expressed as: for every bound M, some finite subset of A has reciprocal sum ≥ M.\n    10→-/\n    11→def DivergentReciprocalSum (A : Set ℕ) : Prop :=\n    12→  ∀ M : ℝ, ∃ F : Finset ℕ, (↑F : Set ℕ) ⊆ A ∧\n    13→    M ≤ ∑ n ∈ F, (1 : ℝ) / (n : ℝ)\n    14→\n    15→/--\n    16→The set A contains an arithmetic progression of length k:\n    17→there exist a, d ∈ ℕ with d ≥ 1 such that {a, a+d, a+2d, …, a+(k-1)d} ⊆ A.\n    18→-/\n    19→def ContainsArithProg (A : Set ℕ) (k : ℕ) : Prop :=\n    20→  ∃ a d : ℕ, 0 < d ∧ ∀ i : ℕ, i < k → a + i * d ∈ A\n    21→\n    22→/--\n    23→Erdős Problem #3 [Er74b, Er75b, Er77c, ErGr79, Er80, Er81, Er83c]:\n    24→\n    25→If A ⊆ ℕ has ∑_{n ∈ A} 1/n = ∞ then A must contain arbitrarily long\n    26→arithmetic progressions.\n    27→\n    28→This is the Erdős conjecture on arithmetic progressions, one of his most\n    29→famous open problems, with a $5000 prize. The case k = 3 was proved by\n    30→Bloom and Sisask (2020).\n    31→-/\n    32→theorem erdos_problem_3\n    33→    (A : Set ℕ)\n    34→    (hA : DivergentReciprocalSum A) :\n    35→    ∀ k : ℕ, ContainsArithProg A k :=\n    36→  sorry\n    37→"}]},"uuid":"65194d62-959e-49d3-a15a-8891ee28fdae","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:17:54.697Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/3.lean","content":"import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Data.Real.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n\nopen BigOperators Finset\n\n/--\nThe set A ⊆ ℕ has divergent reciprocal sum, i.e., ∑_{n ∈ A} 1/n = ∞.\nExpressed as: for every bound M, some finite subset of A has reciprocal sum ≥ M.\n-/\ndef DivergentReciprocalSum (A : Set ℕ) : Prop :=\n  ∀ M : ℝ, ∃ F : Finset ℕ, (↑F : Set ℕ) ⊆ A ∧\n    M ≤ ∑ n ∈ F, (1 : ℝ) / (n : ℝ)\n\n/--\nThe set A contains an arithmetic progression of length k:\nthere exist a, d ∈ ℕ with d ≥ 1 such that {a, a+d, a+2d, …, a+(k-1)d} ⊆ A.\n-/\ndef ContainsArithProg (A : Set ℕ) (k : ℕ) : Prop :=\n  ∃ a d : ℕ, 0 < d ∧ ∀ i : ℕ, i < k → a + i * d ∈ A\n\n/--\nErdős Problem #3 [Er74b, Er75b, Er77c, ErGr79, Er80, Er81, Er83c]:\n\nIf A ⊆ ℕ has ∑_{n ∈ A} 1/n = ∞ then A must contain arbitrarily long\narithmetic progressions.\n\nThis is the Erdős conjecture on arithmetic progressions, one of his most\nfamous open problems, with a $5000 prize. The case k = 3 was proved by\nBloom and Sisask (2020).\n-/\ntheorem erdos_problem_3\n    (A : Set ℕ)\n    (hA : DivergentReciprocalSum A) :\n    ∀ k : ℕ, ContainsArithProg A k :=\n  sorry\n","numLines":37,"startLine":1,"totalLines":37}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0b3933bf-9424-4362-a88b-a57ba912d4d7"}
